a='content/os/02-threads-scheduling/sched-threads.md';b=a.replace('.md','.questions.json');src=['https://go.dev/blog/container-aware-gomaxprocs','https://go.dev/src/runtime/HACKING','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pthreads.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/fork.2.html']
for old,new in [('Start three threads running `work` and each prints `mine=1`, because each has its own `mine`. They all increment the one `hits`. Usually it ends at 3, but because `hits++` is a read, an add and a write, two threads can interleave and lose an update.','Each call has its own `mine`, but the unsynchronised accesses to shared `hits` create a C data race and therefore undefined behaviour: this program has no guaranteed output. The read/add/write lost-update model illustrates one possible failure, not the full language semantics. Use an atomic counter or lock before relying on the output.'),('A segmentation fault, an uncaught signal or a call to `exit()` in any thread terminates the whole process, every thread with it.','A signal with a terminating disposition (such as ordinary default-action SIGSEGV), or a call to exit(), terminates the whole process. Not every uncaught signal has a terminating default action.'),('| Create / switch | Syscall, around µs | Function call, ~100s of ns |','| Create / switch | OS-managed work | Runtime-managed work |'),('fork():   clone() sharing nothing','fork():   private address space'),('`GOMAXPROCS` (by default the number of CPUs available) sets how many can execute Go code at once.','`GOMAXPROCS` limits simultaneous Go execution. Go 1.25 introduced defaults that also consider container CPU limits; explicit settings and compatibility controls can override that policy.'),('so one busy goroutine cannot starve the others.','improving responsiveness for ordinary tight loops; this is not an unconditional scheduling-latency guarantee.'),('Tens of thousands of OS threads are workable; millions are not.','The feasible thread count depends on resource limits, stack configuration and workload.'),('On 8 cores, 8 CPU-bound threads is roughly the useful maximum. More just adds switching.','For CPU-bound work, start from available logical CPUs and measure; SMT, quotas, memory bandwidth and serial work affect the useful concurrency.'),('## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content gap: thread capacity and timing\n> No reproducible benchmark supports universal thread creation/switch timings, maximum thread counts or a fixed Go OS-thread count. These figures are omitted; limits and costs depend on runtime, platform and workload.\n\n## Key takeaways')]:edit(a,old,new,'Correct C data-race semantics, shared resources and runtime/performance assumptions.',src)
d=json.loads(Path(b).read_text());q=next(q for q in d if q['id'].endswith('-q3'))
qe(b,q['id'],'prompt',q['prompt'].replace('int hits = 0;','#include <stdatomic.h>\natomic_int hits = 0;').replace('    hits++;','    atomic_fetch_add(&hits, 1);'),'Remove undefined data race so private-local output has a defined answer.',src)
qe(b,q['id'],'workedExample','Each call has its own automatic mine variable, increments it from 0 to 1 and prints mine=1. The shared hits counter is now atomic, so three completed calls increment it to 3 without a data race. The original non-atomic hits++ would make the entire C program undefined, even though mine itself was not shared.','Do not guarantee print output in a program with unrelated undefined behaviour.',src)
qe(b,q['id'],'explanation','There is no race on mine, and the shared hits update uses an atomic operation. Each call prints its own mine value.','Keep distractor consistent with repaired example.',src,option=3)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q7','workedExample','Both cases save/restore thread state. Sharing an address space can avoid changing memory maps, and shared code/data can preserve useful locality. Translation validity is not guaranteed cache residency; PCID can preserve process translations too. These are possible savings, not a universal ordering of measured switch latency.','Same-process switches do not universally win.',src)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q8','prompt','A worker in a 50-thread process incurs a memory fault that generates SIGSEGV, with its default terminating disposition unchanged. What happens?','Null dereference is undefined in C; state the actual delivered signal.',src)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q8','workedExample','SIGSEGV with its ordinary default action terminates the process, ending all 50 threads. Separate processes provide a memory-isolation boundary, but shared resources and recovery policy can still couple failures; a worker crash does not guarantee that every other service process continues uninterrupted.','Remove overly broad crash-isolation claims about PostgreSQL/browser implementations.',src)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q9','prompt','A 64-bit Linux service requests 10,000 threads, each with an explicitly configured 8 MiB user stack. It has 32 GiB of RAM. Which statement correctly distinguishes virtual reservations from physical memory?','Stack limit is not universally 8MB, and successful creation is not implied.',src)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q9','text','User stacks reserve about 78.125 GiB of address space; physical memory is demand-backed, but creation can still fail because of resource or commit limits','Correct units and remove guaranteed feasibility.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q9','explanation','10,000 × 8 MiB = 80,000 MiB = 78.125 GiB of virtual stack space, excluding guards and other mappings. Actual resident and kernel memory, commit policy and task limits determine feasibility.','Correct virtual-memory arithmetic and necessary limits.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q9','workedExample','User-stack reservations total 78.125 GiB. For an explicitly hypothetical 20 KiB touched user stack plus 16 KiB kernel stack per thread, that subtotal is 360,000 KiB = 351.5625 MiB; task metadata, guards, mappings and other memory add costs. This does not establish that 10,000 creations succeed: check limits, commit policy and workload.','Correct units and avoid unsupported thread-capacity claim.',src)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q11','text','GOMAXPROCS limits simultaneous Go execution to 4; network waiters are parked, but total OS-thread count is not determined by these facts','Total Go thread count is not fixed by GOMAXPROCS or goroutine count.',src,option=2)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q11','explanation','Go can multiplex many network waiters without one OS thread each. Runtime work, blocking calls, cgo and locked threads affect the total; four is an execution-parallelism limit.','Avoid unsupported small fixed number estimate.',src,option=2)
qe(b,'sched-threads-q11','workedExample','Four Ps limit simultaneous ordinary Go execution to four threads. Network goroutines can park on the Linux netpoller without dedicating one thread to each connection. The total thread count also depends on runtime, blocking calls and thread-affinity requirements, so it cannot be calculated from the supplied goroutine count and GOMAXPROCS alone.','Distinguish parallelism from total thread count.',src)
